Description: One-tap USB browse, and stop dropping taps during a PDB parse
 1. USB A/B needed two presses on a freshly inserted stick. The rekordbox
    device list only refreshes on activation, so the first press kicked off an
    async QtConcurrent device scan and returned empty-handed -- doing nothing
    visible -- and only a second press, after the scan had landed, actually
    navigated. This was the most visible "not snappy" moment in the product:
    the appliance looked broken on the very first interaction after boot.
 .
    A press that cannot find its device now records the slot it wanted and
    completes itself from the sidebar model's rowsInserted/modelReset signal
    as soon as the scan reports the stick. The retry deliberately does not
    re-prime the scan (that would restart it on every batch of inserted rows),
    a new press or an eject supersedes any pending request, and a 10 s
    single-shot timeout drops a request for a stick that never appears so it
    can never navigate the sidebar much later.
 .
 2. Tapping a device while a previous PDB parse was still running was silently
    discarded -- correct for the race it guards (concurrent parses would race
    on the shared TreeItem), but on a slow stick that is seconds of UI that
    ignores you with no feedback and no retry. The activation is now queued as
    a QPersistentModelIndex and re-dispatched from onTracksFound() through the
    event loop, so it runs once the future has settled. The queue slot is
    cleared before the result is unwrapped, so a failed parse releases it
    instead of wedging it, and a device removed in the meantime invalidates the
    index rather than dangling.
 .
 Skin side: none -- the USB buttons and their state controls are unchanged.
 Docs: README.md and docs/pi-deploy.md documented the two-press behaviour and
 are updated in the same commit.
Author: Pioneered fork
Forwarded: no

